\ Interpreter-free (`--interpreter-fallback=false`) run of the standalone lint
\ driver. This is a run-and-match fixture: the binary builds, runs over a fixed
\ fixture with ONEZ_STDLIB pointed at the standard library, and prints the same
\ diagnostics as interpreted `1z lint tests/fixtures/lint/unused-import.1z`
\ (the runtime-image `lint_driver.1z` golden), exiting 1 because lint found
\ issues.
\
\ The lexer / lint row-variable words, i.e., `(lexer-step)`, `(try-rules)`,
\ `(lint-rule-with-context)`, all compile. The last one through the
\ interpreter-free runtime-selected dispatch over a symbolic row (`check>>
\ call`). The lint rules live in module-private mutable-map state serialized
\ into the runtime image; the quotations dispatched from it (`check>> call`,
\ the lexer rule table's `match: @get ... call`) are discovered for compilation
\ and their own callees are promoted into the must-compile set, so they carry a
\ real `code_ptr` instead of trapping at runtime with a null one. The lint
\ checks themselves are dispatched as compiled `code_ptr`s.
use "lint" ;
use "lint-rules" ;
{ "tests/fixtures/lint/unused-import.1z" } run-lint [ 0 ] [ 1 ] if sys-exit
